  "description": "This planning application proposes alterations to the existing restaurant. The works include changes to the front and rear façades of the arches, a small single-storey extension, a new internal layout including a mezzanine level, a new access gate to the rear, and the installation of new mechanical equipment to support the restaurant.\n\nIt should be noted that the use class is E, and there is a valid Premises Licence (PREM/2025/0072).\n\nAlterations to the front façades\nThe proposal seeks to better connect the restaurant to the street. It retains the historic London stock brick of the railway arches and sensitively inserts a new lightweight façade to replace the blue roller shutters and windows, and the yellow and blue brickwork. The new façade will be constructed of metallic façade panels and shutters at low level, and a lightweight translucent polycarbonate above head height, providing good light to the space and a more coherent and orderly front façade that addresses the street.\n\nAlterations to the rear\nThe proposal to the rear is to tidy up and make better use of the neglected yard to the rear. The space is currently used to house mechanical ventilation units, large extract units, and condenser units. The proposal is to introduce a new brick boundary wall to replace the galvanised metal fencing, and to inset a lightweight roof structure to cover the space, making it more usable and avoiding it remaining as an anti-social space.\n\nThe proposed brick walls have been kept to an absolute minimum height and will be built in London stock brick to sensitively knit the space into the existing railway arches. The roof is deliberately set back below the line of the brick walls to reduce the impact of the extension. \n\nAlterations to Lamb Lane\nA new entrance facing onto Lamb Lane is proposed. This provides a second means of escape from the restaurant, but will also give the opportunity to tidy up the perimeter wall between the railway arch and No. 31 Lamb Lane. The underpass to the arches and the general condition of the perimeter walls along this section of Lamb Lane feel antisocial and uninviting. Rebuilding this wall and introducing a well-lit, visible entrance will have a positive impact on the street.\n\nInternal arrangement\nThe proposal completely reconfigures the internal arrangement of the restaurant, including a mezzanine level. The restaurant will be a high-end fine dining venue, and the ambition for the interiors will align with this concept. The intention is for the restaurant to have a positive impact on the street and improve the quality of the built fabric of Mentmore Terrace.",
